            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\": \"Distant Gentile nations that are subject to God's severe judgment but are ultimately included in His eschatological grace and the universal outpouring of the Holy Spirit.\",\"primary_basis\": [\"Jeremiah 49:34-39\", \"Acts 2:9\"],\"alternatives\": [{\"meaning\": \"A strictly literal, historical nation with no overarching symbolic meaning beyond its geographic and political role in the Ancient Near East.\",\"basis\": [\"Genesis 10:22\", \"Genesis 14:1\"],\"prominence\": \"dominant\"}, {\"meaning\": \"An instrument of divine judgment and warfare, particularly called by God to judge oppressive empires like Babylon.\",\"basis\": [\"Isaiah 21:2\", \"Isaiah 22:6\"],\"prominence\": \"common\"}, {\"meaning\": \"Eternity or hiddenness, derived from a folk-etymological or mystical association with the Hebrew word 'olam.\",\"basis\": [],\"prominence\": \"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\": false,\"confidence\": \"high\",\"notes\": \"Elam lacks a prominent, universally recognized symbolic meaning in Christian tradition compared to nations like Egypt or Babylon. It is most frequently treated as a strictly historical entity. When interpreted symbolically or typologically, it typically underscores the universal scope of the Gospel, representing the fulfillment of God's promise to eventually restore scattered pagan nations.\"}",
